l to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\); parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis has equation \\(x=c\\); parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIntersecting each of these families with \\(T_3\\) gives  \n\n| line type | equations that meet \\(T_3\\) | points of \\(T_3\\) on the line |\n|-----------|----------------------------|------------------------------|\n| \\(y=c\\)   | \\(y=1\\)                    | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) (3 pts) |\n|           | \\(y=2\\)                    | \\((1,2)\\) (1 pt) |\n|           | \\(y=3\\)                    | \\((1,3)\\) (1 pt) |\n| \\(x=c\\)   | \\(x=1\\)                    | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) (3 pts) |\n|           | \\(x=2\\)                    | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) (2 pts) |\n|           | \\(x=3\\)                    | \\((3,1)\\) (1 pt) |\n| \\(x+y=c\\) | \\(x+y=2\\)                  | \\((1,1)\\) (1 pt) |\n|           | \\(x+y=3\\)                  | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) (2 pts) |\n|           | \\(x+y=4\\)                  | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) (3 pts) |\n\nHence a non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\); the only lines attaining three points are \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).  \nThe only non‑sunny lines containing exactly two points are \\(x=2\\) and \\(x+y=3\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nChecking every unordered pair of points of \\(T_3\\) shows that any line through two points either has one of the prohibited slopes or, in the two admissible cases, contains **exactly** those two points and no third point of \\(T_3\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Case analysis on the non‑sunny line  \n\nLet the unique non‑sunny line be \\(L\\).  \nSince we have three lines in total, the other two lines must be sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1:  \\(|L\\cap T_3| = 3\\)\n\nThe possible choices for \\(L\\)