eads to a contradiction, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must cover all points of \\(T_{3}\\) with three distinct lines, exactly two of which are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non\u2011sunny lines intersecting \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\). Intersecting each family with \\(T_{3}\\) yields:\n\n| family | equations meeting \\(T_{3}\\) | points of \\(T_{3}\\) on the line |\n|--------|----------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| \\(y=c\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) (3 points) |\n|        | \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2)\\) (1 point) |\n|        | \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) (1 point) |\n| \\(x=c\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) (3 points) |\n|        | \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) (2 points) |\n|        | \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) (1 point) |\n| \\(x+y=c\\) | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) (1 point) |\n|          | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) (2 points) |\n|          | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) (3 points) |\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\); the only lines attaining three points are \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).  \nThe only non\u2011sunny lines containing exactly two points are \\(x=2\\) and \\(x+y=3\\).  \nAll other non\u2011sunny lines intersect \\(T_{3}\\) in a single point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny lines contain at most two points\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \