ossibilities are  \n\n\\[\nL:x=2\\quad\\text{(points }(2,1),(2,2)),\\qquad\nL:x+y=3\\quad\\text{(points }(1,2),(2,1)).\n\\]\n\n*Subcase \\(L:x=2\\):* the remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nA sunny line can cover at most two of them, so the two sunny lines must each contain exactly two points.  \nThe only admissible sunny pair among the four points is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nThe two points left, \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), are vertically aligned, which is prohibited for a sunny line. Thus we cannot cover all four points with two sunny lines \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*Subcase \\(L:x+y=3\\):* the remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only admissible sunny pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nThe other two points, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non\u2011sunny, so they cannot be covered by a sunny line. Again three sunny lines would be required \u2013 contradiction.\n\nTherefore \\(|L\\cap T_{3}|=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n---\n\n**Case\u202f3:** \\(|L\\cap T_{3}|=1\\).\n\nAny non\u2011sunny line other than those listed above contains exactly one point of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nSince each sunny line can cover at most two points, the two sunny lines together cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points, leaving at least one point of \\(T_{3}\\) uncovered. Hence this case is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nAll possible forms of the unique non\u2011sunny line lead to contradictions with the requirement that exactly two of the three lines be sunny. Consequently no configuration of three distinct lines covering the set \\(T_{3}\\) can have exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
