            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\).  \nA **non‑sunny** line is therefore horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nWe must decide, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\), whether there exist three distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, whose union contains every point of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\) (all three lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake the three horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad y=3 .\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct, non‑sunny, and\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny=1 &\\text{ contains }(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),\\\\\ny=2 &\\text{ contains }(1,2),(2,2),\\\\\ny=3 &\\text{ contains }(1,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: y = x \\qquad(\\text{sunny, slope }1),\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{2}: x = 1 \\qquad(\\text{vertical, non‑sunny}),\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{3}: y = 1 \\qquad(\\text{horizontal, non‑sunny}).\n\\]\n\nVerification:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1},L_{2},L_{3} \\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1} \\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2} \\\\\n(1,3) & L_{2} \\\\\n(2,1) & L_{3} \\\\\n(3,1) & L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll six points are covered, the three lines are distinct, and exactly one of them (\\(L_{1}\\)) is sunny.  \nHence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=2\\) (two sunny, one non‑sunny) – impossibility\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that such a configuration exists.  \nLet the non‑sunny line be \\(N\\) and the two sunny lines be \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\).\n\n**Observation 1.**  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nIndeed, any three points of \\(T_{3}\\) have either equal \\(x\\)-coordinates (vertical), equal \\(y\\)-coordinates (horizontal), or equal sums \\(x+y\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); thus a line through three of them would be non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently the two sunny lines together cover at most four points, so the non‑sunny line \\(N\\) must contain at least two points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n**Observation 2.**  \nThe non‑sunny lines of \\(T_{3}\\) that contain at least two points are exactly the following seven:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&y=1\\;(3\\text{ points}),\\; y=2\\;(2\\text{ points}),\\\\\n&x=1\\;(3\\text{ points}),\\; x=2\\;(2\\text{ points}),\\\\\n&x+y=3\\;(2\\text{ points}),\\; x+y=4\\;(3\\text{ points}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(Any other horizontal, vertical or diagonal line meets \\(T_{3}\\) in at most one point.)\n\nWe examine each possibility for \\(N\\) and show that the remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n| \\(N\\) | Points on \\(N\\) | Remaining points | Possible sunny pairs among the remainder? |\n|-------|----------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------|\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,