a + (m+1) \\not= m+1-a,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. it is **not** on \\(L_m\\).  Thus \\(L_m\\) covers exactly the two extreme points of the new diagonal.\n\n   The interior points of \\(D_m\\) are now covered by the \\(m-1\\) sunny lines supplied by the induction hypothesis for \\(T_{m-1}\\): each of those lines already contains the point \\((a,m+1-a)\\) for some \\(2\\le a\\le m-1\\) because the construction for \\(T_{m-1}\\) (described below) uses the same family of lines \\(L_{m-1},L_{m-2},\\dots\\).  Consequently after adding \\(L_m\\) we have \\(m\\) sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n   *Explicit description of the inductive construction.*  \n   For every integer \\(r\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_r:\\; y = -\\frac{r-1}{r}\\,x + (r+1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) consists of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines (the slopes are strictly increasing from \\(-0\\) to \\(-1\\) and never equal to \\(-1\\)).  \n   We claim that this family covers \\(T_m\\).  \n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\); thus \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\).  \n   Set \\(r = a+b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le r\\le m\\)).  Substituting \\(r\\) into (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{r-1}{r}\\,x + (r+1)\n     = -\\frac{a+b-2}{a+b-1}\\,x + (a+b).\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating this expression at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a+b-2}{a+b-1}\\,a + (a+b)\n   = \\frac{a+b}{a+b-1}\\;-\\;\\frac{a(a+b-2)}{a+b-1}\n   = b .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).  Because \\(1\\le a+b-1\\le m\\), the line belongs to our family.  This proves that every point of \\(T_m\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\).\n\n   The construction (1) therefore supplies, for **every** \\(m\\ge 1\\), exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * For a given \\(n\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows.  \n\n