            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that meets \\(T_n\\).  \nWrite its equation as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(p\\) rational\n(\\(p=\\frac{u}{v}\\) in lowest terms, \\(v>0\\)).  \nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in T_n\\cap L\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+kv,\\;y_0+ku),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth coordinates must stay positive and satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) are non‑zero, the step \\((v,u)\\) moves at least one unit\nin each direction; consequently the number of admissible values of \\(k\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{\\max\\{|u|,|v|\\}}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequence for the total number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\),\nwith exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nThe \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can be taken, without loss of generality, to be\nvertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); each such line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\nUsing (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge |T_n|\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA short computation gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nk\\le n-1\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, \\(k=n\\) is impossible;\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, (5) does **not** forbid \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nare all parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.\nEach point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and therefore lies on the\ncorresponding line in (6).  This gives a valid configuration for \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  All‑sunny configurations for odd sizes  \n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) be odd (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \nDefine the \\(k\\) lines listed in (3):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_0 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{2i-1} &: y = -\\,i\\,x + (i+1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{2i} &: y = \\frac{1}{i}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{i}\\Bigr),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\n*Points on \\(L_{2i-1}\\).*  \nFor \\(i\\ge1\\) the integer solutions of \\(y=-ix+(i+1)\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,i+1),\\;(2,i),\\;(3,i-1),\\dots ,\\;(i+1,1),\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(i+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n*Points on \\(L_{2i}\\).*  \nThe equation \\(y=\\frac{x}{i}+1-\\frac1i\\) yields the integer points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i-1,2),\\dots ,\\;(1,i),\n\\]\n\nagain \\(i+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n*Points on \\(L_{0}\\).*  \n\\(y=x\\) meets \\(T_{2m+1}\\) in  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots ,\\;(m+1,m+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich are \\(m+1\\) points.\n\nThe sets of points belonging to distinct lines are disjoint, because for a point\n\\((a,b)\\in T_{2m+1}\\) the value \\(a-b\\) uniquely determines which of the equations\nin (7) it satisfies.  Adding the cardinalities we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|T_{2m+1}|=(m+1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(i+1)+(i+1)\n          =(2m+1)(m+1),\n\\]\n\nexactly the size of the triangle.  Hence the \\(k=2m+1\\) sunny lines in (7) partition\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\).  This furnishes a configuration with **all** lines sunny for every odd\nsize \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  From the all‑sunny case to arbitrary \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(0<k\\le n-1\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does\nnot exceed \\(n-k\\).  After deleting those columns the remaining points are\n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a copy of the triangle \\(T_k\\) (simply translate the \\(a\\)-coordinate by\n\\(n-k\\)).  \n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, we apply the all‑sunny construction of Section 4 to \\(T_k\\); this\nproduces exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k'\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2m\\).  First cover \\(T_{2m}\\) by the following\n\\(2m\\) lines:\n\n* the \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines obtained from the odd construction for \\(2m-1\\);\n* one additional non‑sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=2m+1\\).\n\nThe diagonal line supplies the single point \\((1,2m)\\) that is missed by the\n\\(2m-1\\) sunny lines, so all points of \\(T_{2m}\\) are covered and exactly\n\\(2m-1\\) of the lines are sunny.  To obtain **exactly \\(k=2m\\) sunny lines** we\nreplace one of the vertical lines in (8) by a second diagonal line\n\\(x+y=2m+2\\); this adds one more sunny line (the replaced vertical line was\nnon‑sunny) and does not destroy the covering property.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, the construction of Section 4 applied directly with\n\\(k=n\\) gives a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering the whole set \\(T_n\\).\nHence \\(k=n\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility proved in (5) with the explicit constructions of\nSections 3–6 we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of\n\\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{For every }n\\ge3:\\quad 0\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ is possible};\\\\\n&\\text{additionally, }k=n\\text{ is possible iff }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nAll required points are covered, the lines are distinct, and the number of\nsunny lines is exactly the prescribed \\(k\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
