by induction on \\(k\\).*  \n\n   *Base cases.*  \n   – \\(k=1\\): the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) covers the unique point \\((1,1)\\).  \n   – \\(k=2\\): the line \\(y=x\\) covers \\((1,1)\\); a second sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x\\), is distinct and together they cover the whole set \\(T_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.* Assume the statement true for \\(k-2\\) (\\(k\\ge3\\)).  \n   Define two sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_a &: \\text{through }(1,k)\\text{ and }(2,k-2),\\qquad\n          \\text{slope }-2,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_b &: \\text{through }(k,1)\\text{ and }(k-2,2),\\qquad\n          \\text{slope }-\\tfrac12 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Both slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence \\(L_a,L_b\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\n   The integer points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on \\(L_a\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(t,\\,k+2-2t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   and those on \\(L_b\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(t,\\,\\tfrac{k+2}{2}-\\tfrac12 t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Removing all points of \\(L_a\\cup L_b\\) from \\(T_k\\) leaves\n\n   \\[\n   T_k\\setminus(L_a\\cup L_b)=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le k\\}\n   =\\{(a',b')+(1,1)\\mid (a',b')\\in T_{k-2}\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   By the induction hypothesis there are \\(k-2\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k-2}\\); translating each of them by the vector \\((1,1)\\) yields \\(k-2\\) sunny lines covering the set in (8).  \n   Adding the two outer lines \\(L_a,L_b\\) we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\n   The lemma gives a constructive description for every \\(k\\); the construction is explicit, and each integer point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines listed above (either an outer line or a translated inner line).\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n\n   *Existence.*  \n   – For any \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (4) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the lemma for \\(T_k\\). This yields \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points required for the original integer \\(n\\).  \n\n   – If \\(n\\) is odd, the lemma applied with \\(k=n\\) gives an “all‑sunny’’ covering of \\(T_n\\); thus \\(k=n\\) is attainable.  \n\n   – If \\(n\\) is even, the bound (3) forbids \\(k=n\\); therefore no construction is needed.\n\n   *Non‑existence of the missing case.*  \n   When \\(n\\) is odd, suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines existed. Then there would be a single non‑sunny line. Any non‑sunny line is vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\); each of these can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would have to cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  \n   By Lemma 4 each sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points (because \\(n\\) is odd). Hence the total capacity of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n^2-1}{2}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Since  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^2-1}{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2=\\frac{n-1}{2}>0,\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   the counting argument alone does **not** give a contradiction.  \n   A sharper obstruction comes from the two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\). Their connecting line has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore cannot be sunny. Because there is only one non‑sunny line, at most one of the two extreme points can lie on it; the other extreme point must be covered by a sunny line. Hence at least two distinct sunny lines are required for the two extremes. Removing those two sunny lines leaves \\(n-3\\) sunny lines that must cover the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n\\setminus\\{(1,n),(n,1)\\}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1,\\;(a,b)\\neq(1,n),(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating the whole picture by \\((-1,-1)\\) shows that this remaining set is a copy of \\(T_{n-2}\\) together with the point \\((1,1)\\). By the lemma, covering a triangle of size \\(n-2\\) needs **exactly** \\(n-2\\) sunny lines; using only \\(n-3\\) sunny lines is impossible. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n   Therefore the only unattainable value is \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the existence part (steps 4–5) with the impossibility proved above yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(p\\) rational,\n\\(p=\\dfrac{u}{v}\\) in lowest terms (\\(v>0\\)).  \nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in T_n\\cap L\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+kv,\\;y_0+ku),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth coordinates must stay positive and satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore\n\\(|k|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{\\max\\{|u|,|v|\\}}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }\ns:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny, then\n\n\\[\nk\\,s+(n-k)n\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA short computation gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le n,\\qquad\nk\\le n-1\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\); for odd \\(n\\) the bound does not forbid \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the all‑sunny problem  \n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) consider the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and together they contain all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nAfter deleting those columns the remaining points are exactly a translate of\n\\(T_k\\):\n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\{x=i\\}\n   =\\{(a+k',b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_k\\},\n\\qquad k'=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, a configuration for the pair \\((n,k)\\) exists **iff** we can cover\n\\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the “all‑sunny’’ problem).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  All‑sunny covering of a triangle  \n\n**Lemma.** For every integer \\(k\\ge1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\nwhose union contains all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(k\\).*\n\n*Base.*  \n\n* \\(k=1\\): the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) covers the unique point \\((1,1)\\).  \n\n* \\(k=2\\): the line \\(y=x\\) covers \\((1,1)\\); a second sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x\\),\nis distinct. Both together cover the whole set \\(T_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for \\(k-2\\) (\\(k\\ge3\\)).  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_a &: \\text{through }(1,k)\\text{ and }(2,k-2)\n      \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-2x+(k+2),\\\\[2pt]\nL_b &: \\text{through }(k,1)\\text{ and }(k-2,2)\n      \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{k+2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\);\nhence \\(L_a,L_b\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\nThe integer points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on \\(L_a\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,\\,k+2-2t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand those on \\(L_b\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,\\,\\tfrac{k+2}{2}-\\tfrac12 t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nRemoving all points of \\(L_a\\cup L_b\\) from \\(T_k\\) leaves\n\n\\[\nT_k\\setminus(L_a\\cup L_b)=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le k\\}\n   =\\{(a',b')+(1,1)\\mid (a',b')\\in T_{k-2}\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there are \\(k-2\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k-2}\\);\ntranslating each of them by the vector \\((1,1)\\) yields \\(k-2\\) sunny lines\ncovering the set in (11).  Adding the two outer lines \\(L_a,L_b\\) we obtain\n\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\nThe construction is explicit; for every integer point of \\(T_k\\) one can check\nwhether it lies on one of the two outer lines (formulas (9)–(10)) or, after\nsubtracting \\((1,1)\\), belongs to the recursively constructed family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence for the original problem  \n\n*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (6) (non‑sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines\ngiven by the lemma for the triangle \\(T_k\\) (translated by the vector\n\\((n-k,0)\\)).  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one\nof them.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd.*  \nApply the lemma directly with \\(k=n\\); all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a\nvalid configuration.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) even.*  \nImpossible by the bound (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of the missing case \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is odd and a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines\nexists.  Then there is precisely one non‑sunny line \\(M\\).\n\n*Observation 1.*  The two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) cannot lie on\nthe same sunny line because the line through them has slope \\(-1\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) is the **only** non‑sunny line, at most one of the two extreme\npoints can belong to \\(M\\); the other extreme point must be covered by a\nsunny line.  Hence at least two distinct sunny lines are needed for the two\nextremes.\n\n*Observation 2.*  Removing those two sunny lines from the configuration\nleaves \\(n-3\\) sunny lines that must cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus\\{(1,n),(n,1)\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1,\\;(a,b)\\neq(1,n),(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\((1,1)\\) from every remaining point shows that this set is a\ntranslate of the triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) together with the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\nBy the Lemma, covering a triangle of size \\(n-2\\) **requires** exactly\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines; using only \\(n-3\\) sunny lines cannot cover all its\npoints.  Consequently the assumed configuration cannot exist.\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the constructions of Sections 4 and the impossibility proved in\nSection 5 we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{For every }n\\ge3:\\quad 0\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ is attainable};\\\\\n&\\text{additionally, }k=n\\text{ is attainable iff }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\); for\n\\(n=4\\) they are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\); for \\(n=5\\) they are \\(k=0,1,2,3,5\\), etc. ∎"
