\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is the well‑known triangular number.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Upper bound for the number of points on a sunny line\n\n> “Write a sunny line as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(p\\) rational,\n> \\(p=\\dfrac{u}{v}\\) in lowest terms (\\(v>0\\)).  \n> If \\((x_0,y_0)\\in T_n\\cap L\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n> \\[\n> (x_0+kv,\\;y_0+ku),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct.*  For a line of rational slope \\(u/v\\) (coprime) the integer lattice points indeed form an arithmetic progression with step \\((v,u)\\).\n\n> “Both coordinates must stay positive and satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore\n> \\(|k|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{\\max\\{|u|,|v|\\}}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\n> \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n> Hence  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }\n> s:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]”\n\n*Problem.*  The inequality \\(\\max\\{|u|,|v|\\}\\ge2\\) is **not** guaranteed: a sunny line may have slope \\(p=1\\) (i.e. \\(u=v=1\\)), for which \\(\\max\\{|u|,|v|\\}=1\\).  The argument therefore does not rigorously justify the bound \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  Although the bound happens to be true (the sum‑constraint \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) limits the number of points to at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)), the proof given is incomplete.  \n**Classification:** *Justification Gap*.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Capacity bound for a family of lines\n\n> “A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> If a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and exactly\n> \\(k\\) of them are sunny, then  \n> \\[\n> k\\,s+(n-k)n\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct reasoning.*  Since each sunny line can cover at most \\(s\\) points and each non‑sunny line at most \\(n\\) points, the total number of points that can be cov