ique point \\((1,1)\\).  \n> * \\(k=2\\): the line \\(y=x\\) covers \\((1,1)\\); a second sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x\\),\n> is distinct. Both together cover the whole set \\(T_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  The set \\(T_2\\) is **not** \\(\\{(1,1)\\}\\); it contains three points:\n\\[\nT_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\nThe two lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x\\) cover \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) respectively, but they miss \\((2,1)\\).  Hence the base case for \\(k=2\\) is false, and the induction cannot start.\n\n#### 5.2 Inductive step (as written)\n\n> “Assume the statement true for \\(k-2\\) (\\(k\\ge3\\)).  \n> Define  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_a &: \\text{through }(1,k)\\text{ and }(2,k-2)\n>       \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-2x+(k+2),\\\\[2pt]\n> L_b &: \\text{through }(k,1)\\text{ and }(k-2,2)\n>       \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{k+2}{2}.\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]”\n\n*Construction of the two outer lines is correct; both slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) are sunny.*\n\n> “The integer points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on \\(L_a\\) are precisely  \n> \\[\n> \\{(t,\\,k+2-2t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\},\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]  \n> and those on \\(L_b\\) are  \n> \\[\n> \\{(t,\\,\\tfrac{k+2}{2}-\\tfrac12 t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]”\n\n*Issue (Justification Gap).*  Equation (10) omits the parity condition: for a point \\((t,y)\\) to be integer we need \\(k+2-t\\) even, i.e. \\(t\\equiv k\\pmod2\\).  Consequently the set described in (10) contains non‑integer points for half of the listed values of \\(t\\).  The description is therefore inaccurate.\n\n> “Removing all points of \\(L_a\\cup L_b\\) from \\(T_k\\) leaves  \n> \\[\n> T_k\\setminus(L_a\\cup L_b)=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le k\\}\n>    =\\{(a',b')+(1,1)\\mid (a',b')\\in T_{k-2}\\}.\n> \\tag{11}\n> \\]”\n\n*Critical Error.*  The correct inequality after removal is \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), not \\(k\\).  For \\(k=3\\) the right‑hand side of (11) would be empty, yet the point \\((2,2)\\in T_3\\) is not covered by \\(L_a\\) or \\(L_b\\).  Hence (11) is false, and the claimed reduction to a translated copy of \\(T_{k-2}\\) does not hold.\n\n> “By the induction hypothesis there are \\(k-2\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k-2}\\);\n> translating each of them by the vector \\((1,1)\\) yields \\(k-2\\) sunny lines\n> covering the set in (11).  Adding the two outer lines \\(L_a,L_b\\) we obtain\n> \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(T_k\\). ∎”\n\n*Because the induction hypothesis is already false for \\(k=2\\) and the reduction (11) is incorrect, the entire inductive argument collapses.*  \n\n**Conclusion on the Lemma:** The Lemma is false; the proof contains a critical error (false base case) and additional gaps (parity omission, wrong set after removal).  \n\n---\n\n### 6. Using the Lemma to build configurations for the original problem\n\n> “*Case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n> Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (6) (non‑sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n> given by the lemma for the triangle \\(T_k\\) (translated by the vector\n> \\((n-k,0)\\)).  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n> exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one\n> of them.”\n\n*Critical Error.*  This construction **depends** on the Lemma.  Since the Lemma fails already for \\(k=2\\), the construction fails for any instance where the translated triangle is \\(T_2\\).  Concrete counter‑example: \\(n=4,\\;k=2\\).  The vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) leave the translated set \\(\\{(3,1),(3,2),(4,1)\\}\\), which is a copy of \\(T_2\\).  No pair of sunny lines can cover these three points (as shown above), so the claimed configuration does not exist.  Hence the statement “\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable” is false.\n\n> “*Case \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd.*  \n> Apply the lemma directly with \\(k=n\\); all \\(