            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd},\\qquad 0\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\\(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb N\\mid k\\le n,\\;k\\text{ odd}\\,\\}\\).  \nEquivalently, all even \\(k\\ge 2\\) are impossible, while every odd \\(k\\le n\\) (and \\(k=0\\)) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}\\).  \n   \\(|T_m|=m(m+1)/2\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=px+q\\) with rational slope \\(p=u/v\\) in lowest terms,\n   \\(v>0\\), \\(u\\neq0\\) and \\(u\\neq -v\\).  \n   All integer points on the line are of the form \\((x_0+kv,\\;y_0+ku)\\) (\\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).  \n   Moving from one integer point to the next changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by \\(|u|+|v|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Since every point of \\(T_m\\) satisfies \\(x+y\\le m+1\\), at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   integer points of \\(T_m\\) can lie on a sunny line.\n\n3. **Reduction to the “all‑sunny’’ problem.**  \n   Suppose we want a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first\n   coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\).  \n   After deleting those columns, the remaining points are\n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a',b')+(n-k,0)\\mid (a',b')\\in T_k\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a translate of \\(T_k\\).  \n   Consequently, a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) exists **iff** we can cover\n   \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (the “all‑sunny’’ problem).\n\n4. **Covering \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Lemma.* For every odd integer \\(k\\ge1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n   lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Proof by induction on the odd integer \\(k=2m+1\\).*\n\n   *Base \\(k=1\\).*  \n   The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) passes through the unique point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for \\(k-2\\) (which is also odd).  \n   Define two “outer’’ sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_a &: \\text{through }(1,k)\\text{ and }(2,k-2)\n          &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-2x+(k+2),\\\\[2pt]\n   L_b &: \\text{through }(k,1)\\text{ and }(k-2,2)\n          &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{k+2}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) are neither \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence\n   both lines are sunny and clearly distinct.\n\n   **Integer points on the outer lines.**  \n   Because \\(k\\) is odd, the points of \\(L_a\\) with integer coordinates are\n\n   \\[\n   L_a\\cap\\mathbb Z^2=\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(t,\\;k+2-2t\\bigr)\\mid\n          t=1,2,\\dots ,\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   and those of \\(L_b\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   L_b\\cap\\mathbb Z^2=\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(t,\\;\\tfrac{k+2}{2}-\\tfrac{t}{2}\\bigr)\\mid\n          t=1,3,5,\\dots ,k\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (Only odd \\(t\\) give an integer ordinate in (6); the listed set is exactly the\n   integer points of \\(L_b\\).)\n\n   **What remains after removing the outer lines?**  \n   Every point of \\(T_k\\) not belonging to \\(L_a\\cup L_b\\) satisfies\n   \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le k\\); conversely each such point lies on none of\n   the outer lines. Hence\n\n   \\[\n   T_k\\setminus(L_a\\cup L_b)=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le k\\}\n   =\\{(a',b')+(1,1)\\mid (a',b')\\in T_{k-2}\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   By the induction hypothesis there are \\(k-2\\) sunny lines covering\n   \\(T_{k-2}\\). Translating each of them by the vector \\((1,1)\\) yields\n   \\(k-2\\) sunny lines covering the set in (7).  \n   Adding the two outer lines \\(L_a,L_b\\) we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n   covering all of \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\n   The lemma shows that every odd \\(k\\) is feasible for the all‑sunny problem.\n\n5. **Putting the reduction and the lemma together.**  \n\n   *Existence.*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be either \\(0\\) or an odd integer with \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n   * If \\(k=0\\), use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n     non‑sunny and they clearly cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(k\\ge1\\) is odd, take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2) (non‑sunny) and,\n     by the lemma, the \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover the translate of \\(T_k\\)\n     described in (3). The total number of lines is \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them\n     are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n   Hence every \\(k\\) that is \\(0\\) or odd and not larger than \\(n\\) is\n   attainable.\n\n6. **Impossibility of even \\(k\\ge2\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration with an even \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists.\n   By the reduction of step 3 we would obtain a covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   From (1) each sunny line 