            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be fixed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume that a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two\nrequirements of the problem and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\nChoose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines among them to be vertical; after a\nsuitable rotation of the whole picture we may suppose that they are the\nlines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (1), so\nthe only points that still have to be covered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keeping the same \\(b\\), the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na'+b\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConversely, if we can cover the set \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) distinct sunny\nlines, then adding the vertical lines (1) gives a configuration that\nsolves the original problem.  Hence the original problem is equivalent\nto:\n\n> **(P\\(_k\\))**  *Cover the integer points of the triangle \\(T_k\\) by exactly\n> \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A necessary parity condition\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{u}{v}x+q,\\qquad u,v\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;v>0,\\;\\gcd(u,v)=1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith the three forbidden possibilities excluded:\n\\(u=0\\) (horizontal), \\(v=0\\) (vertical), \\(u=-v\\) (parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  All integer points on the line are\n\n\\[\n(x_0+tv,\\;y_0+tu),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAdding the coordinates gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+y_0)+t(u+v).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(|u+v|\\ge1\\).\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the possible sums of the coordinates are\nthe integers  \n\n\\[\n2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFormula (5) shows that, when we move from one lattice point of the line\nto the next, the sum changes by the fixed integer \\(u+v\\).  Consequently\na single sunny line can contain **at most one** point for each value of\nthe sum (6); therefore it can contain at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints.  If \\(k\\) is even, write \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  Each of the\n\\(k\\) lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points, so together they cover at most\n\\(k\\cdot k =4m^{2}\\) points, whereas (7) gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_{2m}|=2m^{2}+m>4m^{2}\\qquad(m\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nThus **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can satisfy (P\\(_k\\))**.  The case \\(k=0\\) is\ntrivial (take no sunny line at all).  Hence a necessary condition is\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=2m+1\\qquad(m\\ge0).\n\\]\n\nDefine three families of lines.\n\n*Central line*  \n\n\\[\nC:\\;y=x .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Family \\(L\\) (slope \\(-2\\))* – for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m\\)\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-2x+(k+2-2i).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Family \\(M\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\))* – for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\)\n\n\\[\nM_i:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac{k+2}{2}-i .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every\nline listed in (9)–(11) is sunny.  The intercepts are pairwise distinct,\nso the lines are distinct.  Counting gives  \n\n\\[\n1+(m+1)+m=2m+1=k\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Verification that the lines cover \\(T_k\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); put  \n\n\\[\ns=a+b\\qquad(2\\le s\\le k+1).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nWe distinguish three mutually exclusive cases.\n\n---\n\n**Case A – \\(a=b\\).**  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the central line \\(C\\) because \\(y=x\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case B – \\(a<b\\).**  \nWrite \\(a=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) (the parity of \\(a\\)).\n\n*Subcase B1 – \\(r=0\\) ( \\(a\\) even).*\n\nSince \\(a<b\\), the sum \\(s\\) is odd; write \\(s=2t+1\\) with\n\\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\ni=m-1-t\\qquad(0\\le i\\le m-1).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (11) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac12a+\\frac{k+2}{2}-i\n   &= -\\frac12(2q)+\\frac{2m+3}{2}-(m-1-t)  \\\\\n   &= -q+m+\\tfrac32-m+1+t                \\\\\n   &= -q+\\tfrac52+t .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s=2t+1=a+b=2q+b\\), we have \\(b=2t+1-2q\\).  Substituting this\ninto the expression above yields exactly \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\nthe equation of \\(M_i\\).\n\n*Subcase B2 – \\(r=1\\) ( \\(a\\) odd).*\n\nNow \\(s\\) is even; write \\(s=2t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni=m+1-t\\qquad(0\\le i\\le m).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nUsing (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2a+(k+2-2i)\n   &= -2(2q+1)+(2m+3-2(m+1-t)) \\\\\n   &= -4q-2+2m+3-2m-2+2t \\\\\n   &= -4q-1+2t .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s=2t=a+b=2q+1+b\\), we have \\(b=2t-2q-1\\), which coincides with the\nright‑hand side above.  Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case C – \\(a>b\\).**  \nThis case is completely symmetric to Case B after exchanging the roles\nof \\(a\\) and \\(b\\); the same calculations show that the point belongs to\na line of family \\(L\\) (if \\(b\\) is odd) or to a line of family \\(M\\)\n(if \\(b\\) is even).\n\n---\n\nAll possibilities have been exhausted, and in each one the point\n\\((a,b)\\) belongs to one of the lines (9)–(11).  Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines listed above cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the original problem\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)* – take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none of\nthem is sunny, and they obviously cover every required point.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\)* – first place the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  The remaining points are exactly the set\n\\(T_k\\).  By the construction of Section 3, \\(T_k\\) is covered by the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines (9)–(11).  Translating those \\(k\\) lines by the vector\n\\((n-k,0)\\) moves the whole picture rightwards so that the vertical lines\nstill cover the points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) and the translated\nsunny lines cover the points with first coordinate \\(\\ge n-k+1\\).  The\ntranslation does not change any slope, hence the lines remain sunny and\nremain distinct.  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least\none of the lines.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\)* – impossible by the parity obstruction\nproved in Section 2.\n\nConsequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\,k\\le n\\mid k\\text{ odd}\\,\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in de