or each\n   sum, i.e. at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\), the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot k\\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   forces \\(k\\) to be odd (or \\(k=0\\)).  Thus a necessary condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd}.\n   \\tag{A}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Explicit construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Define the following \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n   lines (all slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)):\n\n   * Central line \\(C:\\;y=x\\).\n   * Family \\(L\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) \\(L_i:\\;y=-2x+(k+2-2i)\\) for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m\\).\n   * Family \\(M\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) \\(M_i:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\frac{k+2}{2}-i\\) for\n     \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\).\n\n   The intercepts are all integers, hence every line passes through\n   integer points; the three families have pairwise different slopes, so\n   the lines are distinct.  Counting gives \\(1+(m+1)+m=2m+1=k\\) sunny\n   lines.\n\n   **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) lies on one of these lines.**  \n   Put \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)).\n\n   *If \\(a=b\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(C\\).\n\n   *If \\(a<b\\)*, write \\(a=2q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) (parity of \\(a\\)).\n\n   – **Subcase \\(r=0\\) ( \\(a\\) even).**  \n     Then \\(s\\) is odd, say \\(s=2t+1\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le m-1\\).  \n     Define  \n\n     \\[\n     i=m-1-t\\qquad(0\\le i\\le m-1).\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(M_i\\) has equation  \n\n     \\[\n     y=-\\frac12x+\\frac{k+2}{2}-i\n       =-\\frac12x+\\frac{2m+3}{2}-(m-1-t)\n       =-\\frac12x+t+2 .\n     \\]\n\n     Substituting \\(x=a=2q\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     y=-q+t+2 = (2t+1)-2q = s-a = b .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_i\\).\n\n   – **Subcase \\(r=1\\) ( \\(a\\) odd).**  \n     Then \\(s\\) is even, say \\(s=2t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m+1\\).  \n     Define  \n\n     \\[\n     i=m+1-t\\qquad(0\\le i\\le m).\n     \\