\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); otherwise it would miss at least one sum and\n   could contain at most \\(k-1\\) points, contradicting (4).\n\n   Consequently, for a feasible configuration each sunny line must meet\n   the triangle in exactly one point of each sum \\(s\\).  The only slopes\n   that allow this are the two rational slopes whose step in the sum is\n   \\(\\pm1\\), namely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p}{q}=2\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\frac{p}{q}=\\frac12 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(|q-p|\\ge2\\) the sum would skip at least one integer value,\n   contradicting the requirement of \u201cone point per sum\u201d.)\n\n3. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   Assume \\(k\\ge5\\) is odd and that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines covers \\(T_k\\).  By the discussion above each line of \\(\\mathcal\n   L\\) has slope either \\(2\\) or \\(1/2\\).  Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_2=\\{\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid\\text{slope }2\\,\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal L_{1/2}=\\{\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid\\text{slope }1/2\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A line of slope \\(2\\) has equation \\(y=2x+c\\) with integer intercept\n   \\(c\\).  Its points in \\(T_k\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,\\,2x+c)\\quad\\text{with }x\\ge1,\\;2x+c\\ge1,\\;x+(2x+c)\\le k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the admissible \\(x\\) are the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-c}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a slope\u2011\\(2\\) line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-c}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   points of \\(T_k\\).  Because it must contain exactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points,\n   (6) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-c}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2},\n   \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\n   \\frac{k+1}{2}\\le\\frac{k-c}{3}<\\frac{k+1}{2}+1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (7) yields \\(c\\le -\\frac{k+5}{2}\\).  In particular every\n   slope\u2011\\(2\\) line has a **negative** intercept.\n\n   A line of slope \\(1/2\\) has equation \\(y=\\frac12x+d\\) with integer\n   \\(d\\).  Its lattice points are those with even abscissa; writing\n   \\(x=2t\\) we obtain the points \\((2t,\\,t+d)\\).  The condition\n   \\((2t)+(t+d)\\le k+1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-d}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   As before the line must contain \\((k+1)/2\\) points, so (8) forces\n   \\(d\\le -\\frac{k+5}{2}\\); thus every slope\u2011\\(1/2\\) line also has a\n   **negative** intercept.\n\n   Consequently **all** lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) have negative intercepts.\n   But the point \\((1,k)\\in T_k\\) has ordinate \\(k\\ge5\\); substituting\n   \\(x=1\\) into any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) with a negative intercept gives a\n   value \\(\\le 2\\) (for slope \\(2\\)) or \\(\\le 0\\) (for slope \\(1/2\\)),\n   which is far below \\(k\\).  Hence \\((1,k)\\) cannot lie on any line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) \u2013 a contradiction.  Therefore **no** configuration with\n   \\(k\\ge5\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is\n   sunny and every required point has its first coordinate between \\(1\\) and\n   \\(n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the\n   single sunny line \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) is forbidden, so instead take the\n   sunny line \\(y= -2x+(2n+1)\\), which passes through the unique uncovered\n   point \\((n,1)\\).  The line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n   \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); thus we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  After the reduction we have to cover \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),\n   (1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) with three sunny lines.  The following three\n   distinct sunny lines do the job:\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting each of the six points verifies that it lies on at least one\n   of the three lines (the verification is written out explicitly below).\n   Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) gives a total of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of which are sunny.\n\n   Hence configurations exist for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility for all even \\(k\\) and for all odd \\(k\\ge5\\) with\nthe explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be fixed.  \nFor a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) we ask whether there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines in the plane such that  \n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and\n  \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the\n  \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangle\n\nTake \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) is\nalready covered.  The remaining points are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthe integer points of the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(k+1,1),(1,k+1)\\).  Consequently the original problem is\nequivalent to:\n\n> **Triangle problem.**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) decide whether the\n> set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A necessary parity condition\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r ,\\qquad\np,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\;p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\) and \\((x+q,y-p)\\) are two consecutive lattice points on the\nline, then their sums differ by  \n\n\\[\n(x+q)+(y-p)-\\bigl(x+y\\bigr)=q-p .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, moving along the line changes the value of \\(x+y\\) by the constant\n\\(q-p\\).  In particular a sunny line can contain **at most one point for\neach possible sum** \\(s=x+y\\).\n\nIn the triangle \\(T_k\\) the possible sums are the integers  \n\n\\[\n2,3,\\dots ,k+1,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(k\\) different values.  Hence a single sunny line can meet\n\\(T_k\\) in at most \\(k\\) points.\n\nThe cardinality of the triangle is\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{s=2}^{k+1}(s-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf we are allowed to use exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the average number of\npoints per line must be\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|T_k|}{k}= \\frac{k+1}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently **every** sunny line in a feasible configuration must contain\nexactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  In particular \\((k+1)/2\\) must be\nan integer, so **\\(k\\) is odd** (or \\(k=0\\)).  Moreover a line that\ncontains \\((k+1)/2\\) points must meet the triangle in one point for each\nsum \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); otherwise it would miss at least one sum\nand could contain at most \\(k-1\\) points, contradicting (7).\n\nFor a line to hit a point for **every** sum, the step \\(q-p\\) in (4) must\nbe \\(\\pm1\\); any larger absolute value would skip some integer sum.\nThe condition \\(q-p=\\pm1\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) leaves exactly the\ntwo rational slopes\n\n\\[\n\\frac{p}{q}=2\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\frac{p}{q}=\\frac12 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus any line in a feasible configuration has slope either \\(2\\) or\n\\(\\tfrac12\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Impossibility for \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume now that \\(k\\ge5\\) is odd and that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(k\\) sunny lines covers \\(T_k\\).  By the previous paragraph each line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) has slope \\(2\\) or \\(\\tfrac12\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1. Lines of slope\u202f2\n\nA line of slope\u202f2 can be written \\(y=2x+c\\) with an integer intercept\n\\(c\\).  Its lattice points are \\((x,2x+c)\\) with \\(x\\ge1\\) and\n\\(x+(2x+c)\\le k+1\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n1\\le x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-c}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence the line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-c}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(T_k\\).  Because it must contain exactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points,\n(9) forces\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-c}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\n\\frac{k+1}{2}\\le\\frac{k-c}{3}<\\frac{k+1}{2}+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nInequality (10) yields \\(c\\le -\\dfrac{k+5}{2}\\); in particular **every**\nslope\u2011\\(2\\) line has a *negative* intercept.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2. Lines of slope\u202f\\(\\tfrac12\\)\n\nA line of slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) has equation \\(y=\\tfrac12x+d\\) with integer\n\\(d\\).  Its lattice points are those with even abscissa; writing\n\\(x=2t\\) we obtain the points \\((2t,\\,t+d)\\).  The condition\n\\((2t)+(t+d)\\le k+1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n0\\le t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-d}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAgain the line must contain \\((k+1)/2\\) points, so (11) forces\n\\(d\\le -\\dfrac{k+5}{2}\\).  Thus **every** slope\u2011\\(\\tfrac12\\) line also has a\nnegative intercept.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3. Contradiction\n\nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) therefore have negative intercepts.  Consider\nthe point \\((1,k)\\in T_k\\) (recall \\(k\\ge5\\)).  Substituting \\(x=1\\) into\nany line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) gives a value at most\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2\\cdot1 + c \\le 2 -\\dfrac{k+5}{2} < k , &\\text{slope }2,\\\\[4pt]\n\\frac12\\cdot1 + d \\le \\frac12 -\\dfrac{k+5}{2} < k , &\\text{slope }\\tfrac12 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence \\((1,k)\\) cannot lie on any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the\nassumption that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_k\\).  Therefore **no** covering\nwith \\(k\\ge5\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Existence for the remaining values\n\nWe now exhibit explicit configurations for the three values that have not\nbeen ruled out.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.1. \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  None of them is sunny,\nand every required point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on\nthe line \\(x=a\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.2. \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \nThe only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  The line\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=-2x+(2n+1)\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((n,1)\\) because \\(-2n+(2n+1)=1\\).  Its slope \\(-2\\) is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Thus we have\nexactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.3. \\(k=3\\)\n\nAfter the reduction we must cover  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three distinct sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\; y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\ndo the job.  Verification point\u2011by\u2011point:\n\n| point \\((a,b)\\) | lies on which line? |\n|-----------------|----------------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) because \\(1=1\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_1\\) because \\(2=2\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\): \\(-2\\cdot1+5=3\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_2\\): \\(-2\\cdot2+5=1\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(\\ell_3\\): \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52=2\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(\\ell_3\\): \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52=1\\) |\n\nAll six points are covered.  Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) yields a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly three\nof which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe parity argument shows that a feasible configuration can exist only for\n\\(k=0\\) or for odd \\(k\\).  The impossibility proof of \u00a73 eliminates every\nodd \\(k\\ge5\\).  Explicit constructions in \u00a74 demonstrate that the three\nremaining values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are indeed attainable.\n\nHence, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the non\u2011negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. \u220e"

