{6}\n> \\]”\n\n**Assessment (Correct).**  \nThe enumeration of possible sums and the count of lattice points in the right‑angled triangle are accurate.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Average number of points per line  \n\n> **Quote:** “If we are allowed to use exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the average number of points per line must be  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{|T_k|}{k}= \\frac{k+1}{2}.\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]”\n\n**Assessment (Correct).**  \nThe arithmetic is straightforward.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Claim that **every** line must contain exactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points  \n\n> **Quote:** “Consequently **every** sunny line in a feasible configuration must contain exactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  In particular \\((k+1)/2\\) must be an integer, so **\\(k\\) is odd** (or \\(k=0\\)).”\n\n**Issue (Justification Gap).**  \nFrom the average \\((k+1)/2\\) it does **not** follow that each line must contain that many points. An uneven distribution (e.g. some lines with more, some with fewer) is compatible with the average, provided the total number of points is correct and no line exceeds the maximum of \\(k\\) points (one per sum). The solution offers no argument that forces uniformity. Therefore the conclusion that \\(k\\) must be odd (or zero) is not rigorously justified.\n\n---\n\n### 8. “Missing a sum ⇒ at most \\(k-1\\) points”  \n\n> **Quote:** “A line that contains \\((k+1)/2\\) points must meet the triangle in one point for each sum \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); otherwise it would miss at least one sum and could contain at most \\(k-1\\) points, contradicting (7).”\n\n**Issue (Justification Gap).**  \nEven if a line misses a particular sum, it could still contain up to \\(k-1\\) points (one for each of the remaining sums). The inequality \\((k+1)/2 \\le k-1\\) holds for all \\(k\\ge3\\); thus the “contradiction” does not arise. The argument that every line must hit *all* sums is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Determination of possible slopes  \n\n> **Quote:** “For a line to hit a point for **every** sum, the step \\(q-p\\) in (4) must be \\(\\pm1\\); any larger absolute value would skip some integer sum. … The condition \\(q-p=\\pm1\\) together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) leaves exactly the two rational slopes  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{p}{q}=2\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\frac{p}{q}=\\frac12 .\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nEven accepting the (incorrect) step \\(q-p\\), the equation \\(q-p=\\pm1\\) has infinitely many coprime solutions \\((p,q)\\) (any two consecutive integers). Hence there are infinitely many admissible slopes:\n\\[\n\\frac{p}{q}= \\frac{q\\pm1}{q}=1\\pm\\frac1q \\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\frac{p}{q}= \\frac{q\\mp1}{q}=1\\mp\\frac1q .\n\\]\nOnly for \\(q=1\\) do we obtain slopes \\(2\\) or \\(0\\) (the latter excluded), and for \\(q=2\\) we obtain slopes \\(3/2\\) and \\(1/2\\). The claim that *only* \\(2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\) can occur is false. Consequently all later arguments that rely on this restriction (Sections 3.1–3.3) are built on a false premise.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Impossibility for \\(k\\ge5\\) (Sections 3.1–3.3)\n\n> **Quote (excerpt):** “Assume now that \\(k\\ge5\\) is odd and that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covers \\(T_k\\). By the previous paragraph each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) has slope 2 or \\(\\tfrac12\\). … All lines … have negative intercepts … Hence the point \\((1,k)\\) cannot lie on any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradiction.”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nBecause the preceding claim that only slopes \\(2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\) are possible is false, the entire impossibility proof collapses. Moreover, the proof also depends on the unjustified uniformity of point counts per line (see Issue 7). Therefore the conclusion “no covering with \\(k\\ge5\\) sunny lines exists” is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n> **Quote (k=0):** “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).”  \n> **Quote (k=1):** “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and 