            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   After this the only points still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   the integer points of the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n   \\((1,1),(k+1,1),(1,k+1)\\).  \n   Consequently the original problem is *equivalent* to the following\n   **triangle problem**:\n\n   > For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the set\n   > \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct **sunny** lines.\n\n2. **A necessary condition on the slope of a sunny line.**  \n   Write a sunny line in primitive integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   p\\,x-q\\,y=s ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\neq -q .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The direction vector of the line is \\((q,p)\\); moving from one lattice\n   point on the line to the next adds \\((q,p)\\).  \n   Hence the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta:=p+q .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   In the triangle \\(T_k\\) the possible values of the sum are the\n   integers  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\qquad(|S_k|=k).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore a sunny line can meet at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\); the bound is attained only when \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) and\n   the line contains **exactly one point for each sum** in \\(S_k\\).\n\n3. **Parity of \\(k\\).**  \n   If \\(k\\) is even, then \\(|S_k|=k\\) is even.  Because \\(|\\Delta|\\ge1\\),\n   (4) shows that each sunny line contains at most \\(k/2\\) points of\n   \\(T_k\\).  With only \\(k\\) lines the total number of points that can be\n   covered is at most \\(k\\cdot(k/2)=k^{2}/2\\), whereas  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2>\\frac{k^{2}}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no even \\(k\\) works**.\n\n4. **The case \\(k\\ge5\\) (odd).**  \n   Let \\(k\\ge5\\) be odd and suppose that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).\n   By (4) each line must satisfy \\(|\\Delta|=1\\); otherwise the total\n   number of covered points would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil<\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus every line has the form  \n\n   \\[\n   p\\,x-q\\,y=s,\\qquad p+q=\\pm1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and both \\(p,q\\neq0\\), the only possibilities\n   for \\((p,q)\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (p,q)=(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(2,-1),\\;(-1,2),\\;(3,-2),\\;(-2,3),\\dots\n   \\]\n\n   The first two are excluded (they give a horizontal or a vertical line,\n   which is not sunny).  Hence every line has slope either \\(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\n   -\\dfrac{2}{3},-\\dots\\) or the reciprocal slopes \\(2, \\dfrac{3}{2},\n   \\dots\\); in all cases the intercept \\(s\\) is an integer.\n\n   For a line with \\(p+q=1\\) we have \\(p<0<q\\); solving (5) for \\(y\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{p}{q}x+\\frac{s}{q},\n   \\qquad\\text{with }-\\frac{p}{q}>0 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The condition \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) together with \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) yields the\n   bound  \n\n   \\[\n   x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-s}{q-p}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-s}{1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line must contain exactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points (one for each\n   sum in \\(S_k\\)), (7) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k+1}{2}\\le k-s<\\frac{k+1}{2}+1,\n   \\qquad\\text{hence } s\\le -\\frac{k+5}{2}<0 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   An analogous computation for the case \\(p+q=-1\\) gives the same\n   conclusion: **every sunny line has a negative integer intercept**.\n\n   Consider the point \\((1,k)\\in T_k\\) (it exists because \\(k\\ge5\\)).\n   Substituting \\(x=1\\) into any line of the form (5) with a negative\n   intercept yields a value of \\(y\\) that is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   y\\le -\\frac{p}{q}+ \\frac{-\\frac{k+5}{2}}{q}\n        \\le 2 \\;<\\;k .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\((1,k)\\) cannot lie on any of the \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n   contradicting the assumption that the lines cover \\(T_k\\).  Therefore\n   **no odd \\(k\\ge5\\) is possible**.\n\n5. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are\n   non‑sunny and every required point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The\n   only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=-2x+(2n+1)\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   passes through \\((n,1)\\) (since \\(-2n+2n+1=1\\)).  Its slope \\(-2\\) is\n   different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Thus exactly\n   one sunny line is used.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  After the reduction we must cover  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three distinct sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\; y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   do the job.  Verification point‑by‑point:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point}&\\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & \\ell_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & \\ell_1\\\\\n   (1,3) & \\ell_2\\;( -2\\cdot1+5=3)\\\\\n   (2,1) & \\ell_2\\;( -2\\cdot2+5=1)\\\\\n   (1,2) & \\ell_3\\;( -\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52=2)\\\\\n   (3,1) & \\ell_3\\;( -\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52=1)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) yields a total of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of which are sunny.\n\n   Hence configurations exist for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the impossibility results for all even \\(k\\) and for all odd\n\\(k\\ge5\\) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) we obtain the\ncomplete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
