ent as written is false.  \n\n9. **Incorrect solving for\u202fy**  \n   *Quote (Equation\u202f(6)):* \u201cFor a line with\u202fp+q=1\u202fwe have\u202fp<0<q; solving (5) for\u202fy gives\u202f\\(y=-\\frac{p}{q}x+\\frac{s}{q}\\),\u202fwith\u202f\\(-\\frac{p}{q}>0\\).\u201d  \n   *Issue:* **Critical Error.** From\u202fp\u202fx\u202f\u2212\u202fq\u202fy\u202f=\u202fs\u202fwe obtain\u202f\\(y = \\frac{p}{q}x - \\frac{s}{q}\\). The sign before\u202fp/q\u202fis wrong. Consequently all subsequent inequalities that use this expression are based on a false algebraic step.  \n\n10. **Derivation of bound\u202fx\u202f\u2264\u202f\u230a(k\u2212s)/(q\u2212p)\u230b**  \n    *Quote (Equation\u202f(7)):* \u201cThe condition\u202f\\((x,y)\\in T_k\\)\u202ftogether with\u202f\\(x+y\\le k+1\\)\u202fyields the bound\u202f\\(x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-s}{q-p}\\Bigr\\rfloor =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-s}{1}\\Bigr\\rfloor.\\)\u201d  \n    *Issue:* **Critical Error.** This bound is derived from the incorrect expression for\u202fy\u202fand does not follow from the correct relation\u202f\\(y = \\frac{p}{q}x - \\frac{s}{q}\\). The correct inequality would involve\u202f\\(x\\le q(k+1)+s\\)\u202for a similar expression, not the one claimed. Hence (7) is invalid.  \n\n11. **Conclusion about the intercept\u202fs**  \n    *Quote (Equation\u202f(8)):* \u201cBecause the line must contain exactly\u202f\\((k+1)/2\\)\u202fpoints \u2026 (7) forces\u202f\\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\le k-s<\\frac{k+1}{2}+1\\),\u202fhence\u202f\\(s\\le -\\frac{k+5}{2}<0\\).\u201d  \n    *Issue:* **Critical Error.** The inequality in (8) is derived from the false bound (7); therefore the conclusion that every sunny line has a negative integer intercept is unsupported.  \n\n12. **Contradiction using the point\u202f(1,k)**  \n    *Quote:* \u201cConsider the point\u202f\\((1,k)\\in T_k\\)\u202f(it exists because\u202fk\u22655). Substituting\u202fx=1\u202finto any line of the form (5) with a negative intercept yields a value of\u202fy\u202fthat is at most\u202f2\u202f<\u202fk. Consequently\u202f\\((1,k)\\)\u202fcannot lie on any of the\u202fk\u202fsunny lines, contradicting the assumption.\u201d  \n    *Issue:* **Critical Error.** The argument relies on the (incorrect) claim that all sunny lines have a negative intercept (from (8)). Since that claim is unfounded, the derived contradiction does not hold.  \n\n13. **Even\u202fk\u202f>\u202f0 conclusion**  \n    *Quote:* \u201cHence **no even\u202fk works**.\u201d  \n    *Issue:* **Justification Gap.** The statement contradicts the explicit construction for\u202fk=0 (an even\u202fk). The intended claim should be \u201cno even\u202fk\u202f>\u202f0 works\u201d. The proof for even\u202fk\u202f>\u202f0 is already invalid (see point\u202f6), so the wording further obscures the error.  \n\n14. **Existence constructions for\u202fk=0,\u202f1,\u202f3**  \n    *Quote:* (Constructions for\u202fk=0,\u202fk=1,\u202fk=3 are given and verified point\u2011by\u2011point.)  \n    *Analysis:* These constructions are correct:  \n    -\u202fk=0:\u202fn\u202fvertical lines\u202fx=1,\u2026,x=n\u202fcover all required points.  \n    -\u202fk=1:\u202fn\u22121\u202fvertical lines\u202fx=1,\u2026,x=n\u22121\u202fplus the sunny line\u202f\\(y=-2x+(2n+1)\\)\u202fcovers the remaining point\u202f\\((n,1)\\).  \n    -\u202fk=3:\u202fThe three sunny lines\u202f\\(y=x,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\)\u202fcover\u202f\\(T_3\\); adding\u202f\\(n-3\\)\u202fvertical lines completes the set of\u202fn\u202flines.  \n    These parts are rigorous and free of errors.  \n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution\u2019s impossibility arguments for all even\u202fk\u202f>\u202f0 and for all odd\u202fk\u202f\u2265\u202f5 contain several **Critical Errors** (incorrect bounds, false inequalities, algebraic mistakes). Because the final classification\u202f\\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\)\u202frelies on those flawed arguments, the solution does **not** constitute a valid proof. The constructive parts for\u20